{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The St. Louis-San Francisco Railway - the 'Frisco' - the fourth major corridor radiating from St. Louis, running southwest through Springfield, MO to Tulsa and Oklahoma City. Despite the name it never reached San Francisco; it inherited the ambition from the bankrupt Atlantic & Pacific. Route geometry is a simplified set of waypoints through the real cities on the Frisco mainline, NOT taken from any modern Amtrak alignment and not a surveyed track centerline, because no passenger train has run this corridor since 1967 and Amtrak has no equivalent route. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones (construction reaching each town, the 1876 incorporation, named-train introductions, the 1967 end of passenger service); headway_min and avg_speed_kmh are estimates, not from real timetables. Also includes the Frisco's Wichita line, branching from the main line at Pierce City, MO and running through Joplin and Oswego to Wichita (completed 1880) and on to Ellsworth (1888). Its waypoints are the towns the sources name - Pierce City, Sarcoxie, Joplin, Oswego, Fredonia, Beaumont, Wichita, Ellsworth - still hand-placed rather than surveyed track centerline. GEOMETRY UPGRADED 2026-08-18: both features replaced with real-traced geometry from OpenStreetMap active-rail data (Overpass API, shortest-path graph search) -- the corridor is still active BNSF freight trackage today despite carrying no passenger trains since 1967, so it was traceable after all. Main line traced length 873.2 km / 542.6 mi (St. Louis-Springfield-Tulsa-Oklahoma City). Wichita line traced length 546.3 km / 339.4 mi (Pierce City-Joplin-Wichita-Ellsworth); one ~18.5 km stretch just south of Ellsworth is hand-placed since Overpass found no connected path there even including abandoned/disused ways -- likely a fully removed short segment of this minor 1888 extension.", "source": "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.org", "pulled": "20260818", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "St. Louis-San Francisco Railway (Frisco)", "line": "St. Louis-San Francisco: St. Louis \u2013 Oklahoma City", "id": "frisco-stlouis-oklahoma-city", "color": "#B7410E", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 60, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1861, "end": 1876, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Built west from St. Louis as the Pacific Railroad's Southwest Branch, reaching Rolla in 1861 and Springfield, MO in 1870. The Atlantic & Pacific, chartered by Congress in 1866 to build another transcontinental line, pushed from Pierce City into Indian Territory as far as Vinita by 1871, then sat stuck there for a decade against opposition from the Indian Nations. (Full St. Louis-Oklahoma City route shown here regardless of the year each segment opened.) estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1876, "end": 1902, "headway_min": 300, "note": "The St. Louis & San Francisco Railroad was incorporated Sept 7 1876 out of the bankrupt Atlantic & Pacific's Missouri and Central divisions - keeping the transcontinental name it would never live up to. The line finally reached Tulsa in 1882 and Sapulpa in 1885, and the railroad grew into an 'X-shaped' system hubbed at Springfield, MO. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1902, "end": 1940, "headway_min": 240, "note": "The Meteor was inaugurated in 1902 as an overnight St. Louis-Tulsa train, later running through to Oklahoma City; the Frisco's premier trains had a reputation for comfort and fast schedules. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1940, "end": 1948, "headway_min": 180, "note": "The Firefly made its first run Mar 29 1940 on the Kansas City-Tulsa-Oklahoma City route - the Frisco's first streamliner and the first streamliner built in the southwest, with three dramatically streamlined 4-6-2 Pacifics. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1948, "end": 1965, "headway_min": 180, "note": "A streamlined, diesel-powered Meteor began running May 14 1948, and was extended to Lawton, Oklahoma Jul 18 1955. The Firefly ended May 22 1960. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1965, "end": 1967, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Meteor was discontinued in 1965, and the Frisco ended all passenger service in March 1967, becoming freight-only - four years before Amtrak existed, so unlike almost every other line on this map it had no Amtrak successor and this corridor has had no passenger trains since. The Frisco itself was merged into Burlington Northern Nov 21 1980. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-94.8471123, 36.7498316], "to": [-95.0085496, 36.672786], "chord_mi": 10.36, "traced_mi": 10.41, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Cherokee Subdivision (10.41 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 10 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-90.2013521, 38.6225443], [-90.2099531, 38.6245871], [-90.2202737, 38.6245802], [-90.2273065, 38.6263131], [-90.2341374, 38.6291206], [-90.2378678, 38.6296773], [-90.2404187, 38.6289839], [-90.246012, 38.6247651], [-90.2540949, 38.6219822], [-90.2589369, 38.6217095], [-90.267696, 38.6228117], [-90.2831336, 38.6221741], [-90.2878973, 38.6214137], [-90.2936874, 38.6190108], [-90.3051204, 38.6114598], [-90.313838, 38.6011022], [-90.3334767, 38.5936369], [-90.3400464, 38.5880402], [-90.3441726, 38.5860949], 