ouri & Western Railway completed a line from Pierce City, MO through Sarcoxie and Joplin to Oswego, KS in 1879, and the Frisco bought the company that same year. (Full Pierce City-Ellsworth route shown regardless of the year each section opened.) estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1880, "end": 1888, "headway_min": 480, "note": "Between 1872 and 1880 the Frisco built its connecting St. Louis, Wichita & Western from Pierce City on the main line through to Wichita, finishing in 1880 - so Wichita had Frisco service two decades before 1900. Beaumont, KS, previously a stagecoach stop between Fredonia and Wichita, became a Frisco refueling and repair stop that year. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1888, "end": 1890, "headway_min": 420, "note": "Pushed west from Wichita to Ellsworth in 1888 for a connection with the Union Pacific. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1890, "end": 1965, "headway_min": 300, "note": "By 1890 the Frisco was a 1,800-mile network, and Wichita was one of its key western points, with a connection on toward Albuquerque. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}, {"start": 1965, "end": 1967, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Frisco ended all passenger service in March 1967, becoming freight-only four years before Amtrak - so like the rest of the Frisco system this corridor had no Amtrak successor. The railroad merged into Burlington Northern Nov 21 1980. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (St. Louis-San Francisco Railway, Meteor, Firefly), american-rails.com, okhistory.org, missouriencyclopedia.or\u2026"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-97.335801, 37.764138], "to": [-97.4222526, 37.9131353], "chord_mi": 11.33, "traced_mi": 11.34, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["BNSF Arkansas City Subdivision (11.34 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 11 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-94.0005528, 36.9440376], [-94.0089028, 36.9437765], [-94.0111884, 36.9447302], [-94.0122429, 36.946145], [-94.012, 36.9487599], [-94.0059475, 36.9576153], [-94.0060753, 36.959574], [-94.0073204, 36.9611917], [-94.014619, 36.9635346], [-94.0395417, 36.9758145], [-94.065291, 36.9864045], [-94.111306, 37.0333123], [-94.1123044, 37.0353604], [-94.11618, 37.056075], [-94.1179479, 37.0712118], [-94.116599, 37.0867635], [-94.1179186, 37.0893432], [-94.1281613, 37.1017383], [-94.182092, 37.1225796], [-94.2233024